      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Your Monstera is likely suffering from a classic case of \"goblin soil,\" where the dark Seattle soil has become too heavy and waterlogged, causing the roots to drown just like a goblin in a swampy bog. While you're being careful with the water, the low light in Seattle is probably making the plant weak, and the yellowing leaves are a sign that the roots are struggling to breathe, much like a goblin trying to dig a tunnel but getting stuck in mud.\n\nTo fix this, you should let the top inch of soil dry out completely before watering again, ensuring the goblin roots don't get soggy. You might also consider repotting into a lighter mix, perhaps with some perlite, to give the roots more room to expand and avoid the suffocating conditions that goblins love to create. If the leaves continue to droop, it could be a sign of nutrient deficiency, but first, you must address the waterlogging issue, as a drowning plant cannot absorb nutrients no matter how much you add.\n\nRemember, even though you live in the Pacific Northwest, your Monstera needs a bit more sun than the gloomy goblin caves, so try placing it near a bright window to",
